{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>And behold, an olive leaf plucked in her mouth.</b> From the plain sense of this verse it would appear that the trees were not uprooted and were not obliterated by the flood, for there was no rushing torrent there, because the entire world was filled with water. But in Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 33:6) they said: From where did she bring it? Rabbi Levi said: She brought it from the Mount of Anointing, for the land of Israel was not smitten by the waters of the flood — this is what the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Ezekiel: \"Son of man, say to her: You are a land that is not cleansed, not rained upon in the day of indignation\" (Ezekiel 22:24). Rabbi Birei said: The gates of the Garden of Eden were opened for her and she brought it from there. Now, their intention is that the trees were uprooted and obliterated in the places of the flood, and even though the leaf would have been wilted. And so they said (Bereshit Rabbah 30:8): Even millstones were obliterated in the flood, and they expounded upon it: \"The waters wear away the stones\" (Job 14:19). And their statement, \"the land of Israel was not smitten by the waters of the flood,\" means that the rain of the flood did not fall upon it, as it is written, \"not rained upon,\" and the fountains of the great deep were not opened in it; but the waters spread throughout the entire world and covered all the high mountains that are under all the heavens, as is explicitly written (Genesis 7:19), and there is no fence around the land of Israel to hold back the waters from entering it. And thus they said in Pirkei Rabbi Eliezer (chapter 23): Upon the land of Israel the waters of the flood did not descend from the heavens, but the waters rolled from the other lands and entered into it, as it is said: \"Son of man, say to her.\" Now according to the opinion of Rabbi Levi, since the pelting rain did not descend upon that land and the windows of heaven were not opened upon it, the trees remained there, while in all the world they were broken and uprooted by the flood and the torrents of His might. And I wonder at their statement, \"from the Garden of Eden\" — if so, Noah would not have known that the waters had abated from upon the earth, for the waters of the flood did not enter there. Perhaps its gates were closed so that the waters would not enter there, and when the waters abated they were opened. And from this opinion of theirs they said there (Bereshit Rabbah 36:3): \"And he planted a vineyard\" (Genesis 9:20) — and from where did he have it? Rabbi Abba bar Kahana said: When he entered the ark he brought with him cuttings for plantings, shoots of figs, and grafts of olives." "<b>Plucked in her mouth.</b> Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 8:11): I say that it was a male, and therefore Scripture sometimes calls it by the masculine form and sometimes by the feminine form, for every יונה (dove) in Scripture is in the feminine. טרף — snatched. And the midrashic Aggadah: it means food, and they expounded \"בפיה\" (in her mouth) as an expression of speech — she said: let my sustenance be bitter as an olive and in the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, and let it not be sweet as honey in the hand of man. And none of this is correct in my eyes, for switching the gender of the doves from feminine to masculine in one place in the passage has no reason, and if the language is to call all of them by the name of the females, why did it change here? And likewise their midrash in the Aggadah does not actually make \"בפיה\" an expression of speech; rather, their midrash is because she brought this leaf — for if we say it happened thus, Scripture did not specify it for nothing, for it could have said: and behold, a plucked olive leaf in her mouth. And the olive does not come from very tall trees where birds dwell along the length of their branches, and therefore they said that there was a hint in this: that the birds prefer their sustenance to be bitter like wormwood by the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, and not that it be good in the hands of man — and all the more so the children of man, who do not wish to be sustained one from the other. And the language of Bereshit Rabbah (Genesis Rabbah 33:6) is: Rabbi Abbahu said: If she had brought it from the Garden of Eden, she would not have had to bring something superior, or cinnamon, or balsam; rather, she hinted to him: better this, bitter from under the hand of the Holy One, blessed be He, than sweet from under your hand. But in the Gemara they added (Sanhedrin 108b): From where do we infer that this word טרף is an expression of sustenance? For it is written (Proverbs 30:8), \"Feed me (הטריפני) my allotted bread.\" Because of the reason we stated, they made for it a support from the language of \"הטריפני,\" to hint as though it said: and behold, an olive leaf — טרף (food) — in her mouth. And the plain sense of Scripture was explained by them (Onkelos, Ibn Ezra, and Radak) that it is an adjective describing the leaf: and behold, an olive leaf טרוף (torn) in her mouth. And a support for this is that it is vocalized with a qamatz (טָרָף), for such is its grammatical form. But among them are found instances of a different pattern: (Hosea 6:1) \"for He has torn (טָרָף) and He will heal us,\" (Leviticus 5:23) \"and he shall restore the stolen item that he stole (גָּזָל)\", (Leviticus 5:18) \"for his inadvertence that he erred (שָׁגָג)\", and many others besides these."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {0 "In the phrase דְּלָא טָפַת אַרְעָא דְּיִשְׂרָאֵל, the Aramaic טָפַת is rendered literally as 'smitten,' following the Talmudic idiom; the word could also mean 'defiled,' but the context of floodwaters supports the physical sense.", 1 "Ramban argues that טָרָף (with qamatz) is a passive participle ('torn/plucked'), citing as parallel the qamatz forms גָּזָל and שָׁגָג in Leviticus; his reasoning is grammatical, opposing Rashi's derivation from the active verb הטריפני."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:21:20.950700599Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}